WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
A cell that can grip still needs to change shape.

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.420
Shape is the work of actin filaments.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.960
Three small GTPases of the Rho family decide what the filaments build.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.260
They work as Ras does.

5
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.000
Bound to GTP, each one is on.

6
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.040
Bound to GDP, it is off.

7
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.160
GEFs and GAPs set the balance.

8
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.360
In 1992, Anne Ridley and Alan Hall injected purified proteins into fibroblasts.

9
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.480
The cells had been starved of serum, which leaves them with little organised actin.

10
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.760
Active Rho filled the cells with thick actin cables.

11
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.600
Active Rac made their edges ruffle.

12
00:00:52.433 --> 00:01:00.633
In 1995, Catherine Nobes and Alan Hall showed that Cdc42 produces thin spikes.

13
00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:04.913
Separately, the laboratory of Louis Lim showed the same.

14
00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:08.493
So three switches gave three different shapes.

15
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:11.423
Rho builds stress fibres.

16
00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:15.943
These are cables of actin and myosin II that contract.

17
00:01:17.223 --> 00:01:18.803
Rac builds lamellipodia.

18
00:01:19.403 --> 00:01:24.503
These are broad, thin sheets, pushed forward by a branching mesh of filaments.

19
00:01:25.763 --> 00:01:28.323
Cdc42 builds filopodia.

20
00:01:28.803 --> 00:01:32.303
These are thin fingers with a core of parallel filaments.

21
00:01:32.303 --> 00:01:38.843
Keep this rule: each Rho family switch decides which shape the filaments build.
